# {{ .Name }} ({{ .Type | title }})

Get information on a user group resource, including its members, assigned timeframes, profile,
and command restrictions.

## Example Usage

```terraform
# Get information about a specific user group
data "wallix-bastion_usergroup" "developers" {
  group_name = "developers"
}

# Reference the user group in an authorization
resource "wallix-bastion_authorization" "developer_access" {
  authorization_name = "developer-access"
  user_group         = data.wallix-bastion_usergroup.developers.group_name
  target_group       = "web-servers"
}

# Report the number of members in the group
output "developer_count" {
  value = length(data.wallix-bastion_usergroup.developers.users)
}
```

{{ .SchemaMarkdown | trimspace }}

## Usage Notes

### `group_name`

The Required lookup key. It must match an existing user group exactly; the data source fails with
an error if no user group with that name exists.

### `timeframes`

The set of timeframe names that define when members of this group are allowed access (e.g.
`allthetime`, `business_hours`).

### `profile`

The profile name assigned to the group, controlling the GUI/API access level of its members.

### `users`

The set of user names currently belonging to this group.

### `restrictions`

A set of command restrictions applied to sessions for members of this group:

- **action**: Either `kill` (terminate the session) or `notify` (alert only)
- **rules**: The regular expression pattern matched against session activity
- **subprotocol**: The protocol being monitored — common values include `SSH_SHELL_SESSION`,
  `SSH_REMOTE_COMMAND`, `SSH_SCP_UP`, `SSH_SCP_DOWN`, `SFTP_SESSION`, `RDP`, `RLOGIN`, `TELNET`

Since `restrictions` and `users` are sets, do not rely on element order when consuming them.
